  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe nearly stationary boundary that has lingered across the Ohio\nValley will finally begin to move on Saturday, lifting north as\na warm front. This boundary couple with a midlevel shortwave\nwill result in widespread showers pushing across the area\nbeginning Saturday afternoon into the evening. There is a narrow\ncorridor roughly from Findlay to Marion where some strong to\nsevere storms may occur with strong winds the primary threat,\nbut a tornado or two cannot be ruled out. This corridor is the\nmost likely to see these as daytime instability increases\nconcurrent with the synoptic forcing. As the storms slowly\nspread east, areas will gradually begin to lose that daytime\ninstability keeping the severe potential to a minimum. By late\ntomorrow evening, the entire area should see some showers. To\nhighlight the scattered severe potential, SPC has kept the\ncorridor from Marion to Findlay in a Day 2 Slight with a\nMarginal extending further east to a line extending roughly from\nSandusky to Canton. \n\nThe bigger concern with these widespread showers is the very\nheavy and efficient rainfall that is expected to be associated\nwith them. Deepening warm cloud layers combined with PWAT values\nin excess of 2\" falling on already saturate surfaces could\nresult in localized flooding. At times, storm motion may also\nbecoming parallel to mean flow, suggesting the potential for\nisolated training storms to develop which would only enhance the\nflood potential. To highlight this concern, WPC has put the\nentire area (except far NEOH and NWPA) in a Day 2 Slight Risk\nERO. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfter this shortwave and associated surface low move a cold\nfront east on Sunday, high pressure is expected to build east\nover the area and allow for a brief reprieve from precipitation\nacross the area for the start of next week. Chances of\nprecipitation look to increase Wednesday into Thursday as a low\npressure system moves into the region from the central US.\nModels have this midweek system developing into a more robust\nsynoptic trough with more support aloft. Model timing and\npositioning of this low still diverge quite a bit, but this may\nbe another period to keep an eye on for heavy rainfall and the\npotential for strong to severe weather.",
